      "title": "Backup creation is not actually atomic — a crash leaves a partial/empty backup that looks valid",
      "description": "The comment claims the backup is atomic, but `_unique_destination` creates the real destination directory (empty) up front via `mkdir`, then `create_backup` copies into a sibling temp dir and *moves the contents item-by-item into the already-existing destination*. During the entire copy/move window the destination exists and is non-empty-but-incomplete. If the process is killed (or the disk fills) mid-copy or mid-move, the destination remains as a partial or empty directory. `list_backups`/`get_backup` will treat it as a perfectly valid backup. A subsequent `restore_backup` from that partial backup overwrites the live save with incomplete/empty content → silent data loss. The only true-atomic approach is to copytree into the temp sibling and then `rename(temp -> destination)` as the final step, so the destination only ever appears once it is complete.",
